<oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Code>P22</oops:Code><oops:Name>Using different naming conventions in the ontology</oops:Name>
<oops:Description>The ontology elements are not named following the same convention (for example CamelCase or use of delimiters as &quot;-&quot; or &quot;_&quot;) . Some notions about naming conventions are provided in [2].	</oops:Description>
<oops:Importance>Minor</oops:Importance>
<oops:NumberAffectedElements>1</oops:NumberAffectedElements>
<oops:Affects>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000015</oops:AffectedElement>
<oops:AffectedElement>https://bdi.whu.edu.cn/EBM</oops:AffectedElement>
</oops:Affects>
</oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Code>P32</oops:Code><oops:Name>Several classes with the same label</oops:Name>
<oops:Description>Two or more classes have the same content for natural language annotations for naming, for example the rdfs:label annotation. This pitfall might involve lack of accuracy when defining terms.	</oops:Description>
<oops:Importance>Minor</oops:Importance>
<oops:NumberAffectedElements>1</oops:NumberAffectedElements>
<oops:Affects>
<oops:HaveSameLabel>
<oops:AffectedElement>https://bdi.whu.edu.cn/DataSet</oops:AffectedElement>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000100</oops:AffectedElement>
</oops:HaveSameLabel>
</oops:Affects>
</oops:Pitfall>
